== Administration ==
Aizawa functions as a first-level administrative division and serves as the seat of the Aizawa Prefectural Government. In accordance with the national framework for prefectural governance, the executive branch is led by a directly elected mayor, while legislative functions are handled by a municipal assembly. The municipal government operates out of the historic Zong-ku district. The city is broadly divided into eight administrative wards, such as Asuka-ku, which handles the industrial and geothermal sectors, and Ishijima-ku, which serves as the residential and linguistic cultural center. 

The current mayor is {{nihongo|Sato Kenji|佐藤 健二|Satō Kenji}}, a member of the Progressive Party of Okaiken. He has focused his administration on Technocratic Heritage, a policy of preserving historic structures while outfitting them with Satori-integrated smart technology. Sato's primary political rival is the outspoken progressive councilwoman {{nihongo|Ito Maya|伊藤 真耶|Itō Maya}}, a leading figure in the local branch of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]]. She fiercely advocates for the expansion of public housing initiatives, the decommodification of urban spaces, and increased municipal funding for [[National Universal Healthcare Act]] facilities within the city. 

== Culture and Society ==
The archaic polite register of the Ishijima dialect is a point of local pride, with casual service interactions frequently utilizing the formal desu and gozaru forms associated with the medieval court. Aizawa's societal values heavily align with the national Cult of Iron. The local residents deeply embrace structured physical conditioning, with numerous industrial gymnasiums operating along the waterfront where citizens train in heavy compound lifting. This emphasis on physical fortitude is reflected in the city's culinary traditions, which treat the consumption of extremely spicy food as a trial of endurance. Aizawa is a primary producer of volcanic peppers, used extensively in regional variations of Red Offal Stew.

The city has also birthed a unique subset of the national hip-hop scene. Influenced by the rhythmic, mechanical noises of the industrial port and the isolation of the northern islands, local artists have pioneered a distinct coastal variation of the genre. Rising lyricists such as {{nihongo|Hasegawa Ken|長谷川 健}} frequently incorporate the traditional Ishijima dialect into aggressive, bass-heavy tracks that critique the hyper-efficiency of the Satori-managed state.

== Transportation ==
Aizawa serves as the primary logistical and commuter artery for northern Ishijima, boasting a highly integrated transit infrastructure. Public transportation within the city is dominated by the Aizawa Metro. Initially constructed to alleviate surface-level congestion in the densely packed historic districts, it has rapidly expanded to become Okaiken's third-largest subterranean transit network, trailing only the massive systems of Okami and Toyozawa. The metro system is fully integrated with the national Satori algorithm to optimize train frequencies, predict commuter loads during peak industrial shift changes, and manage the complex drainage required for tunneling through a coastal volcanic basin.

Beyond the metro network, the city is a major terminus for the [[Shinkansen|Ishijima Shinkansen]] line, connecting it directly to Okami and Toyozawa via high-speed rail operated by [[Okaiken Railways]]. For aviation, the city is served by Aizawa International Airport, which acts as a secondary hub for [[Okaiken Air]] and facilitates crucial trans-Althenian corridors. The Port of Aizawa remains the commercial lifeblood of the municipality, handling immense volumes of maritime freight and serving as a vital strategic logistics point for the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]].

